Increasing CPU usage for your ONNX runtime application on Android involves several strategies. Before proceeding, please note that high CPU usage can lead to increased battery drain and heat generation, so it's essential to balance performance and resource utilization.

Here are some methods to help you increase CPU usage:

### 1. Multi-Threading

ONNX Runtime supports multi-threading. By default, it uses a single thread. To increase CPU usage, enable multi-threading by setting the `inter_op_num_threads` and `intra_op_num_threads` options when creating an `InferenceSession`.

```java
// Create an inference session with multi-threading
InferenceSession session = new InferenceSession(modelPath, new SessionOptions.Builder()
    .setInterOpNumThreads(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors())
    .setIntraOpNumThreads(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors())
    .build());
```

### 2. Batch Processing

Processing inputs in batches can increase CPU usage. Instead of running inference on individual inputs, group them into batches and pass them through the model.

```java
// Create input tensors with batch size
Tensor inputTensor = Tensor.createFixedSize(TensorShape.create(batchSize, inputSize), DataType.FLOAT32);

// Run inference on batched inputs
session.run(Collections.singletonMap(inputName, inputTensor), outputNames);
```

### 3. Model Optimization

Optimize your ONNX model for mobile devices using techniques like model pruning, quantization, and knowledge distillation. This can reduce computational overhead and increase CPU usage.

### 4. Use a Different Inference Engine

ONNX Runtime provides different inference engines (e.g., `CPU`, `CPU_FP16`, `GPU`). If your device supports it, try using the `GPU` engine to offload computations to the graphics processing unit.

```java
// Create an inference session with GPU engine (if supported)
InferenceSession session = new InferenceSession(modelPath, new SessionOptions.Builder()
    .setExecutionMode(SessionOptions.ExecutionMode.GPU_FP32)
    .build());
```

### 5. Reduce Memory Allocations

Minimize memory allocations and deallocations during inference, as these can lead to performance bottlenecks. Consider reusing input and output tensors.

```java
// Reuse input and output tensors
Tensor inputTensor = Tensor.createFixedSize(inputShape, DataType.FLOAT32);
Tensor outputTensor = Tensor.createFixedSize(outputShape, DataType.FLOAT32);

// Run inference with reused tensors
session.run(Collections.singletonMap(inputName, inputTensor), Collections.singletonList(outputTensor));
```

### 6. Profile and Optimize Hotspots

Use Android Studio's Profiler to identify performance bottlenecks in your application. Focus on optimizing the most CPU-intensive parts of your code.

Remember to monitor CPU usage and adjust these strategies as needed to achieve the desired balance between performance and resource utilization.

### Example Code

Here's a sample code snippet demonstrating some of the above strategies:

```java
// Import necessary libraries
import org.onnxruntime.InferenceSession;
import org.onnxruntime.SessionOptions;
import org.onnxruntime.Tensor;

// Create an inference session with multi-threading and batch processing
InferenceSession session = new InferenceSession(modelPath, new SessionOptions.Builder()
    .setInterOpNumThreads(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors())
    .setIntraOpNumThreads(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors())
    .build());

// Create input tensors with batch size
Tensor inputTensor = Tensor.createFixedSize(TensorShape.create(batchSize, inputSize), DataType.FLOAT32);

// Run inference on batched inputs
session.run(Collections.singletonMap(inputName, inputTensor), outputNames);

// Reuse input and output tensors
Tensor outputTensor = Tensor.createFixedSize(outputShape, DataType.FLOAT32);
session.run(Collections.singletonMap(inputName, inputTensor), Collections.singletonList(outputTensor));
```

Note: Replace `modelPath`, `inputName`, `inputSize`, `batchSize`, `outputShape`, and `outputNames` with your actual model and inference settings.

