Yes, in Node.js, you can implement a pull-based stream using the `Readable` stream class. Here's an example of how you can create a simple pull-based stream:

```javascript
const { Readable } = require('stream');

class PullBasedStream extends Readable {
  constructor(options) {
    super(options);
    this.data = ['item1', 'item2', 'item3'];
    this.index = 0;
  }

  _read(size) {
    if (this.index >= this.data.length) {
      this.push(null); // signal the end of the stream
    } else {
      const item = this.data[this.index++];
      this.push(item); // push the item into the stream
    }
  }
}

// Usage:
const stream = new PullBasedStream();
stream.on('data', (chunk) => {
  console.log(`Received chunk: ${chunk}`);
});

stream.on('end', () => {
  console.log('Stream ended');
});
```

In this example, the `PullBasedStream` class extends the `Readable` stream class and overrides the `_read` method. The `_read` method is called when the stream is ready to receive more data. It checks if there is more data to be read and if so, it pushes it into the stream using the `push` method. If there is no more data, it signals the end of the stream by pushing `null`.

The `stream.on('data')` event is emitted when the stream receives a chunk of data, and the `stream.on('end')` event is emitted when the stream ends.

However, the `Readable` stream in Node.js is actually a push-based stream under the hood. The `_read` method does not actually pull data into the stream, but rather signals the stream to start pushing data.

To create a truly pull-based stream in Node.js, you can use a library like `pull-stream`, which provides a pull-based stream implementation.

Here's an example of how you can create a pull-based stream using `pull-stream`:

```javascript
const pull = require('pull-stream');

const data = ['item1', 'item2', 'item3'];
let index = 0;

function read(abort, cb) {
  if (abort) {
    return cb(true);
  }

  if (index >= data.length) {
    return cb(true);
  }

  const item = data[index++];
  cb(null, item);
}

function sink(read) {
  read(null, (end, chunk) => {
    if (end) {
      console.log('Stream ended');
    } else {
      console.log(`Received chunk: ${chunk}`);
      sink(read);
    }
  });
}

sink(read);
```

In this example, the `read` function is a pull-based stream source, and the `sink` function is a pull-based stream sink. The `sink` function calls the `read` function to pull data into the stream, and the `read` function returns the data or signals the end of the stream.

