You can use the `Swoole\Coroutine` or `Fiber` in PHP 8.2 for managing coroutines, but PHP doesn't have a built-in way to limit the number of running coroutines. You can achieve this by using a semaphore.

Here's an example using `Fiber` and a semaphore to limit the number of running coroutines:

```php
class CoroutineManager
{
    private $maxConcurrency;
    private $semaphore;
    private $tasks = [];

    public function __construct(int $maxConcurrency)
    {
        $this->maxConcurrency = $maxConcurrency;
        $this->semaphore = new Semaphore($maxConcurrency);
    }

    public function addTask(callable $task): void
    {
        $this->tasks[] = $task;
    }

    public function run(): void
    {
        foreach ($this->tasks as $task) {
            $this->semaphore->acquire();
            Fiber::async($task)->start();
        }
    }
}

class Semaphore
{
    private $slots;
    private $mutex;

    public function __construct(int $slots)
    {
        $this->slots = $slots;
        $this->mutex = new Mutex();
    }

    public function acquire(): void
    {
        $this->mutex->lock();
        while ($this->slots <= 0) {
            $this->mutex->wait();
        }
        --$this->slots;
        $this->mutex->unlock();
    }

    public function release(): void
    {
        $this->mutex->lock();
        ++$this->slots;
        $this->mutex->notify();
        $this->mutex->unlock();
    }
}

class Mutex
{
    private $mutex;

    public function __construct()
    {
        $this->mutex = \fopen(__FILE__, 'r');
    }

    public function lock(): void
    {
        \flock($this->mutex, LOCK_EX);
    }

    public function unlock(): void
    {
        \flock($this->mutex, LOCK_UN);
    }

    public function wait(): void
    {
        $cond = new Cond($this->mutex);
        $cond->wait();
    }

    public function notify(): void
    {
        $cond = new Cond($this->mutex);
        $cond->notify();
    }
}

class Cond
{
    private $cond;

    public function __construct($mutex)
    {
        $this->cond = $mutex;
    }

    public function wait(): void
    {
        \stream_select([$this->cond], [], [], null);
    }

    public function notify(): void
    {
        \fread($this->cond, 1);
        \fwrite($this->cond, '1');
    }
}

$manager = new CoroutineManager(5);

for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
    $manager->addTask(function () use ($i) {
        echo "Task $i started\n";
        Fiber::suspend();
        // Simulate some work
        Fiber::sleep(1);
        echo "Task $i finished\n";
        // Release the semaphore
        $GLOBALS['manager']->semaphore->release();
    });
}

$manager->run();

// Restart the fiber after suspend
foreach ($manager->tasks as $i => $task) {
    Fiber::async($task)->resume();
}
```

However, the code above is a bit tricky and complex because of the lack of built-in support for coroutines in PHP.

However you can achieve that in much easier way if you use the `amphp` or `revoltphp` libraries which are providing an event loop that can limit the number of running coroutines.

Here's an example using `amphp`:

```php
use Amp\Loop;
use Amp\Parallel\Worker\Pool;

$loop = Loop::get();

$pool = new Pool(5);

for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
    $pool->submit(function () use ($i) {
        echo "Task $i started\n";
        // Simulate some work
        usleep(1000000);
        echo "Task $i finished\n";
    });
}

$loop->run();
```

And here's an example using `revoltphp`:

```php
use Revolt\EventLoop;

$loop = EventLoop::get();

$semaphore = new \Revolt\EventLoop\Semaphore(5);

for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
    $semaphore->wait();
    $loop->spawn(function () use ($i, $semaphore) {
        echo "Task $i started\n";
        // Simulate some work
        usleep(1000000);
        echo "Task $i finished\n";
        $semaphore->notify();
    });
}

$loop->run();
```

To prevent XPath injection in Java, you can use a parameterized XPath expression. Unfortunately, Java's built-in `XPath` class does not support parameterized expressions out-of-the-box. However, you can use a workaround by compiling an XPath expression with variables, and then setting the variables separately.

Here is an example of how to do this using the `XPath` and `XPathConstants` classes:

```java
import javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpressionException;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;

import org.w3c.dom.Document;

public class XPathExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create an XPath instance
        XPath xPath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();

        // Compile an XPath expression with variables
        String expression = "/users/user[@name=$name and @pass=$pass]";

        // Create a Document
        DocumentBuilderFactory factory = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        Document document = factory.newDocumentBuilder().parse("users.xml");

        // Set the variables
        xPath.setXPathVariableResolver(new CustomVariableResolver(user, pass));

        // Evaluate the XPath expression with the variables
        try {
            Object result = xPath.evaluate(expression, document, XPathConstants.NODE);
            if (result != null) {
                System.out.println("User found");
            } else {
                System.out.println("User not found");
            }
        } catch (XPathExpressionException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid XPath expression");
        }
    }
}

class CustomVariableResolver implements javax.xml.xpath.XPathVariableResolver {
    private String name;
    private String pass;

    public CustomVariableResolver(String name, String pass) {
        this.name = name;
        this.pass = pass;
    }

    @Override
    public Object resolveVariable(QName variableName) {
        if (variableName.getLocalPart().equals("name")) {
            return name;
        } else if (variableName.getLocalPart().equals("pass")) {
            return pass;
        } else {
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we create an `XPath` instance and compile an XPath expression with variables. We then create a `CustomVariableResolver` instance that resolves the variables to their corresponding values. The `CustomVariableResolver` class implements the `XPathVariableResolver` interface, which is used to resolve variables in the XPath expression.

By using this approach, you can prevent XPath injection attacks by ensuring that the variables are properly escaped and validated.

Alternatively, you can also use a library such as Apache Commons JXPath, which provides built-in support for parameterized XPath expressions.

```java
import org.apache.commons.jxpath.JXPathContext;
import org.apache.commons.jxpath.xml.DocumentContainer;

public class XPathExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create a DocumentContainer
        DocumentContainer container = new DocumentContainer("users.xml");

        // Create a JXPathContext
        JXPathContext context = JXPathContext.newContext(container);

        // Declare variables
        context.declareVariable("name", user);
        context.declareVariable("pass", pass);

        // Evaluate the XPath expression with the variables
        try {
            Object result = context.selectSingleNode("/users/user[@name=$name and @pass=$pass]");
            if (result != null) {
                System.out.println("User found");
            } else {
                System.out.println("User not found");
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid XPath expression");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we create a `DocumentContainer` instance that loads an XML document from a file. We then create a `JXPathContext` instance that provides a context for evaluating XPath expressions. We declare variables using the `declareVariable` method, and then evaluate the XPath expression using the `selectSingleNode` method.

**Strategy Pattern in JavaScript: Banking App System**
======================================================

The strategy pattern allows you to define a family of algorithms, encapsulate each one, and make them interchangeable. In this example, we'll create a banking app system that uses different payment strategies.

**Payment Strategies**
--------------------

We'll define three payment strategies: `CreditCard`, `PayPal`, and `BankTransfer`.

```javascript
// Strategy interface
class PaymentStrategy {
  pay(amount) {
    throw new Error("Method must be implemented");
  }
}

// Concrete strategy: CreditCard
class CreditCard extends PaymentStrategy {
  constructor(name, cardNumber, cvv, expiryDate) {
    super();
    this.name = name;
    this.cardNumber = cardNumber;
    this.cvv = cvv;
    this.expiryDate = expiryDate;
  }

  pay(amount) {
    console.log(`Paid $${amount} using credit card ${this.cardNumber}`);
  }
}

// Concrete strategy: PayPal
class PayPal extends PaymentStrategy {
  constructor(email) {
    super();
    this.email = email;
  }

  pay(amount) {
    console.log(`Paid $${amount} using PayPal account ${this.email}`);
  }
}

// Concrete strategy: BankTransfer
class BankTransfer extends PaymentStrategy {
  constructor(accountNumber) {
    super();
    this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
  }

  pay(amount) {
    console.log(`Paid $${amount} using bank transfer to account ${this.accountNumber}`);
  }
}
```

**Banking App System**
----------------------

Now, let's create the banking app system that uses these payment strategies.

```javascript
// Context class: BankingApp
class BankingApp {
  constructor(paymentStrategy) {
    this.paymentStrategy = paymentStrategy;
  }

  setPaymentStrategy(paymentStrategy) {
    this.paymentStrategy = paymentStrategy;
  }

  executePayment(amount) {
    this.paymentStrategy.pay(amount);
  }
}
```

**Example Usage**
----------------

```javascript
// Create payment strategies
const creditCard = new CreditCard("John Doe", "1234-5678-9012-3456", "123", "12/2025");
const paypal = new PayPal("john.doe@example.com");
const bankTransfer = new BankTransfer("1234567890");

// Create banking app with initial payment strategy
const bankingApp = new BankingApp(creditCard);

// Execute payment using credit card
bankingApp.executePayment(100);

// Switch to PayPal payment strategy
bankingApp.setPaymentStrategy(paypal);
bankingApp.executePayment(200);

// Switch to bank transfer payment strategy
bankingApp.setPaymentStrategy(bankTransfer);
bankingApp.executePayment(300);
```

Output:

```
Paid $100 using credit card 1234-5678-9012-3456
Paid $200 using PayPal account john.doe@example.com
Paid $300 using bank transfer to account 1234567890
```

This code demonstrates the strategy pattern in JavaScript using a banking app system. The payment strategies are encapsulated in separate classes, making it easy to add or modify strategies without affecting the rest of the system.

Here's a draft program that uses the stack frame concept to add 150 numbers generated by the "memorygenerate" macro.

```arm
.macro memorygenerate DESTINATION, SIZE
    mov r0, #1
    ldr r1, =\DESTINATION

    loop\@:
        str r0, [r1]
        add r1, #4
        add r0, #1

        cmp r0, #\SIZE
        ble loop\@
.endm

.data
    .align 4
    myArray: .space 600
    sumOfarray: .word 0

.text

.global main
main:
    // Generate the array
    memorygenerate myArray, 151 // I changed it to 151 because you want numbers from 1 to 150

    // Initialize the stack pointer
    mov r0, #0
    mov r1, #0
    mov r2, #0
    mov r3, #0
    mov r4, #0
    mov r5, #0

    // Initialize the base address of the array
    ldr r0, =myArray

    // Initialize the counter
    mov r1, #0

    // Loop through the array and push elements to the stack
loop_start:
    // Load the element from the array
    ldr r2, [r0]

    // Push the element to the stack
    push {r2}

    // Increment the counter
    add r1, #1

    // Increment the base address
    add r0, #4

    // Check if the counter is less than or equal to 150
    cmp r1, #150
    ble loop_start

    // Initialize the counter again
    mov r1, #0

    // Loop through the stack and add elements using the addsubroutine
loop_add:
    // Check if there are at least two elements in the stack
    cmp r1, #149
    bgt end_loop

    // Call the addsubroutine
    bl addsubroutine

    // Increment the counter
    add r1, #1

    b loop_add

end_loop:
    // The final sum is the only element left in the stack
    pop {r0}

    // Store the sum in the sumOfarray variable
    ldr r1, =sumOfarray
    str r0, [r1]

    // Exit the program
    mov r0, #0
    mov pc, lr

// Subroutine to add the two last pushed values in the stack and store it in the location of the second value in the stack
addsubroutine:
    // Pop the last two elements from the stack
    pop {r0}
    pop {r1}

    // Add the two elements
    add r0, r1

    // Push the result back to the stack
    push {r0}

    // Return to the caller
    bx lr
```

This program generates the array using the "memorygenerate" macro, pushes all elements to the stack, and then adds them using the "addsubroutine". The final sum is stored in the "sumOfarray" variable. Note that this program assumes that the stack is initialized properly and that there is enough space in the stack to hold all elements. Also, this program uses the `bx lr` instruction to return from the subroutine, which is the standard way to return from a subroutine in ARM assembly language.

**PERN Stack To-Do List App**
================================

This is a basic implementation of a To-Do List app using the PERN stack (PostgreSQL, Express, React, Node.js).

**Backend (Node.js, Express, PostgreSQL)**
-----------------------------------------

### Dependencies

* `express`: ^4.17.1
* `pg`: ^8.7.1
* `dotenv`: ^10.0.0

### Database Setup

Create a new PostgreSQL database and add the following table:

```sql
CREATE TABLE tasks (
  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
  description TEXT,
  completed BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
);
```

### `server.js`

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const { Pool } = require('pg');
require('dotenv').config();

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const pool = new Pool({
  user: process.env.DB_USER,
  host: process.env.DB_HOST,
  database: process.env.DB_NAME,
  password: process.env.DB_PASSWORD,
  port: process.env.DB_PORT,
});

// Get all tasks
app.get('/tasks', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const results = await pool.query('SELECT * FROM tasks');
    res.json(results.rows);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    res.status(500).json({ message: 'Error fetching tasks' });
  }
});

// Create a new task
app.post('/tasks', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { title, description } = req.body;
    const result = await pool.query(
      'INSERT INTO tasks (title, description) VALUES ($1, $2) RETURNING *',
      [title, description]
    );
    res.json(result.rows[0]);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    res.status(500).json({ message: 'Error creating task' });
  }
});

// Update a task
app.put('/tasks/:id', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { id } = req.params;
    const { title, description, completed } = req.body;
    const result = await pool.query(
      'UPDATE tasks SET title = $1, description = $2, completed = $3 WHERE id = $4 RETURNING *',
      [title, description, completed, id]
    );
    res.json(result.rows[0]);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    res.status(500).json({ message: 'Error updating task' });
  }
});

// Delete a task
app.delete('/tasks/:id', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { id } = req.params;
    await pool.query('DELETE FROM tasks WHERE id = $1', [id]);
    res.json({ message: 'Task deleted successfully' });
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    res.status(500).json({ message: 'Error deleting task' });
  }
});

const port = process.env.PORT || 3001;
app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server listening on port ${port}`);
});
```

