Here's a recursive JavaScript function that retries a request 3 times before it resolves the promise:

```javascript
function retryRequest(url, maxAttempts = 3, attempt = 0) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    fetch(url)
      .then((response) => {
        if (!response.ok) {
          throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
        }
        resolve(response);
      })
      .catch((error) => {
        if (attempt < maxAttempts - 1) {
          console.log(`Request failed. Retrying... (Attempt ${attempt + 1} of ${maxAttempts})`);
          retryRequest(url, maxAttempts, attempt + 1)
            .then(resolve)
            .catch(reject);
        } else {
          console.log(`Request failed after ${maxAttempts} attempts. Giving up.`);
          reject(error);
        }
      });
  });
}

// Example usage:
retryRequest('https://example.com/api/data')
  .then((response) => response.json())
  .then((data) => console.log(data))
  .catch((error) => console.error(error));
```

In this code, the `retryRequest` function sends a request using the `fetch` API and checks if the response is OK. If not, it throws an error. If an error occurs, the function checks if the maximum number of attempts has been reached. If not, it calls itself recursively with the same URL and incremented attempt number. If the maximum number of attempts has been reached, it rejects the promise with the error.

