Below is a simple Rust program that reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from other URLs, and downloads the YouTube URLs using yt-dlp.

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use std::process::Command;

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    // Read URLs from file
    let file = File::open("urls.txt")?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    let mut youtube_urls = Vec::new();
    let mut other_urls = Vec::new();

    for line in reader.lines() {
        let line = line?;
        let url = line.trim();

        // Separate YouTube URLs
        if url.contains("youtube.com") || url.contains("youtu.be") {
            youtube_urls.push(url.to_string());
        } else {
            other_urls.push(url.to_string());
        }
    }

    println!("YouTube URLs:");
    for url in &youtube_urls {
        println!("{}", url);
    }

    println!("Other URLs:");
    for url in &other_urls {
        println!("{}", url);
    }

    // Download YouTube URLs using yt-dlp
    for url in &youtube_urls {
        let output = Command::new("yt-dlp")
            .arg(url)
            .output()?;

        if output.status.success() {
            println!("Downloaded {}", url);
        } else {
            println!("Failed to download {}: {}", url, String::from_utf8_lossy(&output.stderr));
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

Please make sure to have `yt-dlp` installed on your system. Also, replace `"urls.txt"` with your actual file path.

**Note:** This program uses `std::process::Command` to execute `yt-dlp` command. It assumes that `yt-dlp` is installed and available in the system's PATH.

**Error Handling:** The program uses `Result` to handle errors. It will print error messages if there are any issues while reading the file, executing the `yt-dlp` command, or downloading the videos.

